The general eligibility criteria for **CAP/DA services under NC Medicaid** are:

1. **The person must be enrolled in NC Medicaid**, and their eligibility category cannot include restrictions that make them ineligible for CAP/DA services. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 2.1.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025, current)

2. **The requested CAP/DA service must meet NC Medicaid’s general coverage standards**. It must be:
   - individualized, specific, and consistent with the person’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and not more than the person needs;
   - safely furnished, with no equally effective and more conservative or less costly statewide alternative available; and
   - furnished in a way that is **not primarily for the convenience** of the beneficiary, caretaker, or provider. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025, current)

3. **The person must meet CAP/DA-specific criteria**, including:
   - meeting the required **HCBS nursing facility level of care (LOC)** as determined by NC Medicaid, with long-term care support needs typically provided in an institution and related to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
   - having a completed CAP assessment showing a reasonable indication that the person will need at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within 30 calendar days of the evaluation;
   - having a **signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis, or short-term installation of equipment/modification/technology to help the person remain in or return to a primary private residence and avoid institutional placement;
   - being able to maintain health, safety, and well-being in the primary private residence or another approved location using formal and informal supports;
   - having paid care needs that can be maintained **within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
   - having a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
   - having an **emergency backup and disaster plan** for care disruptions. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025, current)

4. **Age/scope context:** the policy’s case-management entity requirements refer to experience serving **adults 18 years of age and older** with medical complexities or physical disabilities, which is consistent with CAP/DA being an adult waiver program. ([Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 6.4.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025, current)

## In short
A person generally qualifies for CAP/DA if they are an **NC Medicaid beneficiary**, the services meet Medicaid’s **general medical-necessity/coverage rules**, and they meet the waiver’s **specific institutional level-of-care, assessment, service-plan, home-safety, cost, physician-coordination, and backup-plan requirements**. The current authoritative policy is **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, effective September 1, 2025**.

Under NC Medicaid’s **Orthotics & Prosthetics** policy, the specifically **not covered** items are:

- **Devices for the beneficiary’s comfort or convenience**, or for the convenience of the beneficiary’s caregiver(s)
- **Backup devices** or **duplicate devices** kept at different locations
- **Devices and supplies for residents of nursing facilities**
- **Devices or supplies covered by another agency**
- **Equipment or supplies for beneficiaries receiving hospice care** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 4.2.1]

There are also broader **general exclusions**. NC Medicaid does not cover the item or service if:

- the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does not meet the coverage criteria in Section 3.0;
- the procedure, product, or service duplicates another provider’s procedure, product, or service; or
- the procedure, product, or service is experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 4.1].

## General criteria that must be met for coverage

For orthotics or prosthetics to be covered, the policy says they must:

- be **ordered by a physician, physician assistant, or nurse practitioner**;
- be **medically necessary** to maintain or improve the beneficiary’s medical, physical, or functional level;
- be **appropriate for use in a noninstitutional setting** where normal life activities take place;
- be supported by a **documented face-to-face encounter** with the ordering physician, PA, or NP related to the primary reason for the device, occurring **no more than 6 months before initiation** of the orthotics/prosthetics; and
- have the beneficiary’s need **reviewed at least annually** by the ordering physician, PA, or NP [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 3.2.1; § 3.2].

The policy adds that:

- **Orthotic devices** are medically necessary when required to **correct or prevent skeletal deformities, support or align movable body parts, or preserve or improve physical function**.
- **Prosthetic devices** are medically necessary when they **replace all or part of the function of a permanently inoperative, absent, or malfunctioning body part**, and the beneficiary must require the prosthesis for **mobility, daily care, or rehabilitation purposes** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 3.2.1].

In addition, the devices must be:

- a **reasonable and medically necessary** part of the treatment plan;
- **consistent with the beneficiary’s diagnosis and medical condition**, especially functional limitations and symptoms; and
- **of high quality**, with replacement parts available and obtainable [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), § 3.2.1].

## Documentation-related criteria

The policy’s Attachment E restates that the request must involve a device that:

- fits the definition of an orthotic or prosthetic;
- is for an eligible NC Medicaid beneficiary;
- is requested by an enrolled provider with the proper taxonomy;
- is **safe, effective, economical**, and **not for convenience**;
- is **medical in nature**, generally accepted, and **not experimental or investigational** [[Orthotics & Prosthetics](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/5b-orthotics-prosthetics/download?attachment), Attachment E].

I checked for amendments to Policy **5B**, and the knowledge base identifies **Orthotics & Prosthetics** as the current authoritative document, with no listed change notices amending it in the retrieved record. If you want, I can also pull out how this policy handles **prior authorization** or **adult unlisted/non-covered items under Attachment E**.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must have a **written treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved within the duration of the plan**;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature of the professional completing the treatment plan**, dated **on or before the start date** of the treatment plan. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) (effective 2025-02-01), § 3.2.1.6.

For documentation, Policy 10A also requires the record to include **a copy of the treatment plan** as part of the service documentation. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) (effective 2025-02-01), § 7.2.

If helpful, I can also pull the related **order requirement** and explain how the treatment plan differs from the provider order and from prior authorization documentation under 10A.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to an identified beneficiary need and be part of the person-centered/service planning process. The beneficiary must work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for “equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services,” and the modification must serve CAP/DA purposes such as improving mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. Current authority is the 9/1/2025 version of Policy 3K-2, with no separate amendment returned in the amendment record. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

CAP/DA allows home modifications for a beneficiary’s primary private residence:
- if the residence is owned by the beneficiary or family;
- if it is a rental and the modification is portable; or
- if it is a rental and the owner allows the modification.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Covered examples listed in the policy include:
- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways/passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- medically necessary emergency egress in some circumstances;
- bathroom modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- certain flooring, lifts, door modifications, vehicle-related adaptations, wheelchair tie-downs, hoists, ramps, and other approved modifications;
- some smart-home and air-filtration items when the policy’s conditions are met.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The service must be aimed at one or more of these policy goals:
- improving, maximizing, or enhancing mobility, safety, or independence;
- improving integration into the community;
- improving environmental and community accessibility; or
- addressing the beneficiary’s 24-hour/7-day coverage concerns.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment/modification/technology requests, the MDT documentation must include:

- **A recommendation by the MDT** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification.
- **The cost of the item/modification** in the recommendation.
- **A plan for training** the beneficiary and family in use of the equipment/modification when applicable; that training must be documented by the case manager as completed and signed by the beneficiary or responsible party.
- **A statement of need in the service plan.**
- **Evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable.
- **Itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request.
- **Any other information required for the specific item requested.**
- **Required quotes** for purchase/adaptation/modification when NC Medicaid requires them.
- **Written recommendations from appropriate professionals** if NC Medicaid or its designee determines they are needed because of special circumstances.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also specifically lists supporting documentation for equipment, supplies, adaptations, or modifications, including:
- **a comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment completed by the case manager** identifying the equipment/supply/adaptation/modification needs;
- **a copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- **a recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need regarding the requested modification;
- **the estimated life of the item** and **how long the beneficiary is expected to benefit** from it; and
- **an invoice from the supplier** showing the date provided, cost, and related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Practical takeaway

For a CAP/DA home modification request, the MDT should make sure the file shows:
1. the modification is for the beneficiary’s qualifying residence;
2. the modification addresses a documented functional/safety/access need;
3. the need is reflected in the service plan;
4. medical necessity/support is documented when applicable;
5. cost/quotes/proposal details are included; and
6. the case manager’s multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment and any required professional or physician documentation are in the record.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

