Physicians should provide Medicaid-covered fluoride varnish **from tooth eruption until the beneficiary is 3½ years old**. NC Medicaid also limits billing for fluoride varnish to beneficiaries **under 3½ years of age**, **once every 60 calendar days**, and **no more than six times before the child reaches 3½**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), eff. Dec. 4, 2019, §§ 7.2, 5.3.2]

## Oral screening requirements

NC Medicaid’s physician fluoride varnish policy requires the oral screening to include:

- **Early caries screening and detection of notable findings** in the oral cavity, including obvious hard- and soft-tissue pathology, **using a dental mirror and directed light**;
- **Counseling and educational materials** on good oral hygiene practices and nutrition for children;
- **Prescribing a fluoride supplement, if indicated**, under the policy’s cited pediatric guidelines;
- **Application of fluoride varnish** to all erupted primary teeth beginning at eruption and continuing until age 3½. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), eff. Dec. 4, 2019, § 7.2]

## Required documentation tied to the screening

The beneficiary’s health record must document:

- the **oral evaluation** and any notable findings;
- **preventive oral health and dietary counseling** with the primary caregiver;
- the **application of fluoride varnish**; and
- **referral to a dentist, if appropriate**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), eff. Dec. 4, 2019, § 7.2]

## Current-policy note

I checked for amendments to this policy, and the tool returned **no amendments**, with **Clinical Coverage Policy 1A-23, Physician Fluoride Varnish Services** as the authoritative current document. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), eff. Dec. 4, 2019]

Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet and maintain specific certification standards, and NC Medicaid may decertify the provider if it fails those standards or commits certain compliance violations.

## Certification requirements

A provider of HIV Case Management must:

- have a documented successful record of **3 years** providing or managing HIV Case Management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are delivered by **qualified case managers** under the policy’s staff qualification rules;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** under the policy’s supervisor qualification rules;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records** and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days** and no later than **5 business days** after the actual change; and
- achieve **national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2, 6.2.1 (effective 2020-01-15; no amendments returned)

The recertification checklist adds practical application requirements for recertification submissions, including that the provider identify:

- all case managers and supervisors with dates of employment;
- all counties where HIV CM services are provided;
- all services provided by the agency;
- operating hours and an emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- the frequency of beneficiary satisfaction surveys and how results are used; and
- the numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current; no amendments returned)

The policy also reflects broader application/certification expectations, including administrative and program requirements such as a physical business site verified by site visit, organizational chart, ownership information, business plan, computer capability for Medicaid and HIPAA compliance, a precertification onsite visit, and written agency policies including confidentiality, grievance, rights, nondiscrimination, conflict of interest, records, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation policies. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) a. Administrative Requirements; b. Case Management Requirements (effective 2020-01-15; no amendments returned)

## When a provider can be decertified

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide core service components;
- fraudulent billing practices;
- owner conviction of a felony;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** for unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- falsification of records;
- violation of beneficiary confidentiality;
- employing staff who do not meet policy qualifications;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory basic training within **90 days** of employment;
- failure of staff to obtain required continuing education units;
- failure to provide staff supervision adequate to beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within required time frames;
- failure to provide documentation sufficient to support billing, including documentation required by policy section 7.5;
- failure to implement and enforce a quality improvement program;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid/designated contractor of agency changes, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable federal and state Medicaid laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and policies; 
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months** of certification; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in section 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 6.2.5 (effective 2020-01-15; no amendments returned)

## Related compliance points that matter in practice

Because decertification grounds include documentation failures and quality failures, these sections are especially important:

- Required documentation includes intake forms, assessments, care plans, progress notes, consent forms, physician/practitioner orders showing diagnosis and medical necessity, documentation of contacts, refusals, and progress toward goals. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 7.5
- Time frames include initial assessment within **5 business days** of referral, care plan within **5 business days** after assessment, progress notes within **24 hours** of contact, quarterly care plan review, and at least annual beneficiary satisfaction survey. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 7.6
- NC Medicaid or its contractor may conduct quality assurance site visits reviewing staff qualifications, documented training, case management services, chart documentation, complaints, and quality improvement activities. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) § 7.8

